engine.Run needs to guarantee two processes never write into the same torrent's DataDir concurrently, even in the case where the parent dies between spawning a child (cmd.Start()) and recording that child's pid into config.json -- a later resume can't tell from config.json alone whether an orphaned first child is still running, so it could spawn a second one into the same directory. store.ReconcileLiveness's pid/boot_id check only catches that after the fact, once two processes are already writing into the same files; it can't prevent the race itself.

Run instead calls process.AcquireLock as its very first action, before even loading config.json: an exclusive, non-blocking flock (syscall.LOCK_EX|syscall.LOCK_NB) on a per-torrent lock file, wrapped so contention surfaces as the sentinel process.ErrLockHeld rather than a generic I/O error. flock is a kernel-held lock, released automatically the instant the holding process's file descriptors close -- for any reason, including a clean exit, a crash, kill -9, or a machine reboot -- so a second Run either gets the lock or gets ErrLockHeld, with no pid-reuse or 'is it really dead' inference required.

Considered leaning harder on the existing pid/boot_id liveness check (tighter timing, writing the pid earlier) instead of adding a second mechanism, but that only narrows the window between spawn and pid-write rather than closing it -- flock closes it directly because the guarantee comes from the kernel, not from a value on disk that could be stale or reused.
